  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday) \nIssued at 131 AM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThe Pacific storm system off the California coast will eject inland \ninto The Great Basin on Thursday, with very strong southerly \nmoisture advection out ahead of it. Model consensus now keeps the \nmain vort max well off to the northwest of New Mexico, which will \nfocus the main bulk of precipitation over eastern Arizona early \nThursday, eventually pushing into western New Mexico later in the \nday. PWATs will peak early Thursday across northwest NM at 4 to 5 \nstandard deviations above normal for mid-October, shattering record \nlevels for this time of year. Therefore, confidence is very high for \nsoaking moderate rainfall across the Four Corners area, but \ninstability remains a big question mark. Guidance has trended \nslightly more stable, keeping the steeper mid-level lapse rates off \nto the south and east over southern Arizona and central New Mexico. \nEven with the absence of robust instability, rounds of moderate \nrainfall over an extended period could create flooding issues for \nsensitive low-lying areas that cannot handle large volumes of water.\n\nStrong vertical wind shear associated with a 700 mb jet max will \npromote the development of strong storms on Thursday afternoon and \nwould not be surprised if there are a few supercells near the \nArizona border as low-level winds shift around to the southeast and \nprovide some directional shear as well. Given recent storm track \nshifts and the uncertainty with regard to exactly where corridors of \nelevated instability reside leave a big question mark around where \nthe flash flood threat will be maximized. Furthermore, it should be \nnoted that the flood threat will not necessarily follow the typical \ndiurnal cycle with the threat peaking during the afternoon hours and \ndiminishing through the night. Pockets of elevated nocturnal \ninstability could keep moderate to heavy rates around through the \novernight hours throughout this event.\n\nWhile the energy from the main trough will exit off to the northeast \nFriday, deep Pacific moisture will remain entrenched over New \nMexico. All 3 deterministic global models now favor the development \nof another Low over Arizona, which would keep large scale ascent and \nmoisture over New Mexico through the weekend, with rounds of showers \nand storms night and day. Ensemble guidance is trending this way as \nwell, although around 50% of models still favor an open wave trough \nmoving over central NM this weekend. With either scenario, rain \nchances will focus over along and east of the central mountain \nchain, as a backdoor front and Gulf moisture interact over the\narea.",
